      "title": "Financing and delivery in one organization",
      "narration": "A health maintenance organization does two things that insurers and hospitals normally do separately. It collects a fixed prepaid amount for each member, and it arranges or directly provides the care those members need. Because it holds the money and runs the care, it has every incentive to keep members healthy and to manage how services are used. An HMO operates in a defined service area; members who travel outside it are generally covered only for emergencies and urgent care. In Maryland an HMO is authorized under the Health-General Article, holds a certificate of authority from the Insurance Commissioner and answers to the Department of Health on quality of care.",
